G-API is a separate OpenCV module so its header files have to be included explicitly. The first four lines of create and initialize OpenCV's standard video capture object, which fetches video frames from either an attached camera or a specified file.
G-API pipeline is constructed next. In fact, it is a series of G-API operation calls on [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph.") data. The important aspect of G-API is that this code block is just a declaration of actions, but not the actions themselves. No processing happens at this point, G-API only tracks which operations form pipeline and how it is connected. G-API _Data objects_ (here it is [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph.")) are used to connect operations each other. `in` is an _empty_ [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph.") signalling that it is a beginning of computation.
After G-API code is written, it is captured into a call graph with instantiation of [cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") object. This object takes input/output data references (in this example, `in` and `out` [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph.") objects, respectively) as parameters and reconstructs the call graph based on all the data flow between `in` and `out`.
[cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") is a thin object in sense that it just captures which operations form up a computation. However, it can be used to execute computations – in the following processing loop, every captured frame (a [cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class") `input_frame`) is passed to [cv::GComputation::apply()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html#aa25bc73f7cba9bb2f9992edaa248f868 "Compile graph on-the-fly and immediately execute it on the inputs data vectors.").
Example pipeline running on sample video 'vtest.avi'
[cv::GComputation::apply()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html#aa25bc73f7cba9bb2f9992edaa248f868 "Compile graph on-the-fly and immediately execute it on the inputs data vectors.") is a polimorphic method which accepts a variadic number of arguments. Since this computation is defined on one input, one output, a special overload of [cv::GComputation::apply()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html#aa25bc73f7cba9bb2f9992edaa248f868 "Compile graph on-the-fly and immediately execute it on the inputs data vectors.") is used to pass input data and get output data.
Internally, [cv::GComputation::apply()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html#aa25bc73f7cba9bb2f9992edaa248f868 "Compile graph on-the-fly and immediately execute it on the inputs data vectors.") compiles the captured graph for the given input parameters and executes the compiled graph on data immediately.
There is a number important concepts can be outlines with this example:
  * Graph declaration and graph execution are distinct steps;
  * Graph is built implicitly from a sequence of G-API expressions;
  * G-API supports function-like calls – e.g. [cv::gapi::resize()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d91/group__gapi__transform.html#gad0ae87ad8b909c3c11a07b007837be8b "Resizes an image."), and operators, e.g operator|() which is used to compute bitwise OR;
  * G-API syntax aims to look pure: every operation call within a graph yields a new result, thus forming a directed acyclic graph (DAG);
  * Graph declaration is not bound to any data – real data objects ([cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class")) come into picture after the graph is already declared.


See [tutorials and porting examples](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/d7e/tutorial_table_of_content_gapi.html) to learn more on various G-API features and concepts.

