##  Automatic Memory Management
OpenCV handles all the memory automatically.
First of all, std::vector, [cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class"), and other data structures used by the functions and methods have destructors that deallocate the underlying memory buffers when needed. This means that the destructors do not always deallocate the buffers as in case of Mat. They take into account possible data sharing. A destructor decrements the reference counter associated with the matrix data buffer. The buffer is deallocated if and only if the reference counter reaches zero, that is, when no other structures refer to the same buffer. Similarly, when a Mat instance is copied, no actual data is really copied. Instead, the reference counter is incremented to memorize that there is another owner of the same data. There is also the [cv::Mat::clone](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a03d2a2570d06dcae378f788725789aa4 "Creates a full copy of the array and the underlying data.") method that creates a full copy of the matrix data. See the example below: 
// create a big 8Mb matrix
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) A(1000, 1000, [CV_64F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga30a562691cc5987bc88eb7bb7a8faf2b));
// create another header for the same matrix;
// this is an instant operation, regardless of the matrix size.
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) B = A;
// create another header for the 3-rd row of A; no data is copied either
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) C = B.[row](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a4b22e1c23af7a7f2eef8fa478cfa7434)(3);
// now create a separate copy of the matrix
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) D = B.[clone](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a03d2a2570d06dcae378f788725789aa4)();
// copy the 5-th row of B to C, that is, copy the 5-th row of A
// to the 3-rd row of A.
B.[row](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a4b22e1c23af7a7f2eef8fa478cfa7434)(5).[copyTo](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a33fd5d125b4c302b0c9aa86980791a77)(C);
// now let A and D share the data; after that the modified version
// of A is still referenced by B and C.
A = D;
// now make B an empty matrix (which references no memory buffers),
// but the modified version of A will still be referenced by C,
// despite that C is just a single row of the original A
B.[release](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#ae48d4913285518e2c21a3457017e716e)();
// finally, make a full copy of C. As a result, the big modified
// matrix will be deallocated, since it is not referenced by anyone
C = C.[clone](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a03d2a2570d06dcae378f788725789aa4)();
CV_NODISCARD_STD Mat clone() const
Creates a full copy of the array and the underlying data.
void copyTo(OutputArray m) const
