#  G-API Kernel API
The core idea behind G-API is portability – a pipeline built with G-API must be portable (or at least able to be portable). It means that either it works out-of-the box when compiled for new platform, _or_ G-API provides necessary tools to make it running there, with little-to-no changes in the algorithm itself.
This idea can be achieved by separating kernel interface from its implementation. Once a pipeline is built using kernel interfaces, it becomes implementation-neutral – the implementation details (i.e. which kernels to use) are passed on a separate stage (graph compilation).
Kernel-implementation hierarchy may look like:
Kernel API/implementation hierarchy example
A pipeline itself then can be expressed only in terms of `A`, `B`, and so on, and choosing which implementation to use in execution becomes an external parameter.
#  Defining a kernel
G-API provides a macro to define a new kernel interface – [G_TYPED_KERNEL()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/de/d7f/gkernel_8hpp.html#a83d2e73c619a545f69e89bf7b03cbd80):
#include <[opencv2/gapi.hpp](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d7/d20/gapi_8hpp.html)>
[G_TYPED_KERNEL](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/de/d7f/gkernel_8hpp.html#a83d2e73c619a545f69e89bf7b03cbd80)(GFilter2D,
<[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)([cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html),int,[cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html),[cv::Point](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/d4e/classcv_1_1Point__.html),double,int,[cv::Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/da0/classcv_1_1Scalar__.html))>,
"org.opencv.imgproc.filters.filter2D")
{
static [cv::GMatDesc](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d0/d82/structcv_1_1GMatDesc.html) // outMeta's return value type
outMeta([cv::GMatDesc](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d0/d82/structcv_1_1GMatDesc.html) in , // descriptor of input GMat
int ddepth , // depth parameter
[cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) /* coeffs */, // (unused)
[cv::Point](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/d4e/classcv_1_1Point__.html) /* anchor */, // (unused)
double /* scale */, // (unused)
int /* border */, // (unused)
[cv::Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/da0/classcv_1_1Scalar__.html) /* bvalue */ ) // (unused)
{
return in.[withDepth](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d0/d82/structcv_1_1GMatDesc.html#a964bd20bd0f7be0b171a13e256e637ab)(ddepth);
}
};
This macro is a shortcut to a new type definition. It takes three arguments to register a new type, and requires type body to be present (see [below](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d0/d25/gapi_kernel_api.html#gapi_kernel_supp_info)). The macro arguments are:
  1. Kernel interface name – also serves as a name of new type defined with this macro;
  2. Kernel signature – an `std::function<>`-like signature which defines API of the kernel;
  3. Kernel's unique name – used to identify kernel when its type informattion is stripped within the system.


Kernel declaration may be seen as function declaration – in both cases a new entity must be used then according to the way it was defined.
Kernel signature defines kernel's usage syntax – which parameters it takes during graph construction. Implementations can also use this signature to derive it into backend-specific callback signatures (see next chapter).
Kernel may accept values of any type, and G-API _dynamic_ types are handled in a special way. All other types are opaque to G-API and passed to kernel in `outMeta()` or in execution callbacks as-is.
Kernel's return value can _only_ be of G-API dynamic type – [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph."), [cv::GScalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/d98/classcv_1_1GScalar.html "GScalar class represents cv::Scalar data in the graph."), or . If an operation has more than one output, it should be wrapped into an `std::tuple<>` (which can contain only mentioned G-API types). Arbitrary-output-number operations are not supported.
Once a kernel is defined, it can be used in pipelines with special, G-API-supplied method "::on()". This method has the same signature as defined in kernel, so this code:
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) out = GFilter2D::on(/* GMat */ in,
/* int */ -1,
/* Mat */ conv_kernel_mat,
/* Point */ [cv::Point](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/d4e/classcv_1_1Point__.html)(-1,-1),
/* double */ 0.,
/* int */ [cv::BORDER_DEFAULT](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#gga209f2f4869e304c82d07739337eae7c5afe14c13a4ea8b8e3b3ef399013dbae01),
/* Scalar */ [cv::Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/da0/classcv_1_1Scalar__.html)(0));
is a perfectly legal construction. This example has some verbosity, though, so usually a kernel declaration comes with a C++ function wrapper ("factory method") which enables optional parameters, more compact syntax, Doxygen comments, etc:
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) [filter2D](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html#ga27c049795ce870216ddfb366086b5a04)([cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) in,
int ddepth,
[cv::Point](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/d4e/classcv_1_1Point__.html) anchor = [cv::Point](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/d4e/classcv_1_1Point__.html)(-1,-1),
double scale = 0.,
int border = [cv::BORDER_DEFAULT](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#gga209f2f4869e304c82d07739337eae7c5afe14c13a4ea8b8e3b3ef399013dbae01),
[cv::Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/da0/classcv_1_1Scalar__.html) bval = [cv::Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/da0/classcv_1_1Scalar__.html)(0))
{
return GFilter2D::on(in, ddepth, k, anchor, scale, border, bval);
}
so now it can be used like:
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) out = [filter2D](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html#ga27c049795ce870216ddfb366086b5a04)(in, -1, conv_kernel_mat);
#  Extra information
In the current version, kernel declaration body (everything within the curly braces) must contain a static function `outMeta()`. This function establishes a functional dependency between operation's input and output metadata.
_Metadata_ is an information about data kernel operates on. Since non-G-API types are opaque to G-API, G-API cares only about `G*` data descriptors (i.e. dimensions and format of [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph."), etc).
`outMeta()` is also an example of how kernel's signature can be transformed into a derived callback – note that in this example, `outMeta()` signature exactly follows the kernel signature (defined within the macro) but is different – where kernel expects [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph."), `outMeta()` takes and returns [cv::GMatDesc](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d0/d82/structcv_1_1GMatDesc.html) (a G-API structure metadata for [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph.")).
The point of `outMeta()` is to propagate metadata information within computation from inputs to outputs and infer metadata of internal (intermediate, temporary) data objects. This information is required for further pipeline optimizations, memory allocation, and other operations done by G-API framework during graph compilation.
#  Implementing a kernel
Once a kernel is declared, its interface can be used to implement versions of this kernel in different backends. This concept is naturally projected from object-oriented programming "Interface/Implementation" idiom: an interface can be implemented multiple times, and different implementations of a kernel should be substitutable with each other without breaking the algorithm (pipeline) logic (Liskov Substitution Principle).
Every backend defines its own way to implement a kernel interface. This way is regular, though – whatever plugin is, its kernel implementation must be "derived" from a kernel interface type.
Kernel implementation are then organized into _kernel packages_. Kernel packages are passed to [cv::GComputation::compile()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html#ab11bcc0578f9aa5ec8f8eaf6cda0dfa8 "Compile the computation for specific input format\(s\).") as compile arguments, with some hints to G-API on how to select proper kernels (see more on this in "Heterogeneity"[TBD]).
For example, the aforementioned `Filter2D` is implemented in "reference" CPU (OpenCV) plugin this way (_NOTE_ – this is a simplified form with improper border handling):
#include <[opencv2/gapi/cpu/gcpukernel.hpp](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/d73/gcpukernel_8hpp.html)> // GAPI_OCV_KERNEL()
#include <[opencv2/imgproc.hpp](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d4f/imgproc_2include_2opencv2_2imgproc_8hpp.html)> // cv::filter2D()
[GAPI_OCV_KERNEL](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/d73/gcpukernel_8hpp.html#aacef2b3c16c285adbd70a03ae8aedc46)(GCPUFilter2D, GFilter2D)
{
static void
run(const [cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) &in, // in - derived from GMat
const int ddepth, // opaque (passed as-is)
const [cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) &k, // opaque (passed as-is)
const [cv::Point](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/d4e/classcv_1_1Point__.html) &anchor, // opaque (passed as-is)
const double delta, // opaque (passed as-is)
const int border, // opaque (passed as-is)
const [cv::Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/da0/classcv_1_1Scalar__.html) &, // opaque (passed as-is)
[cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) &out) // out - derived from GMat (retval)
{
[cv::filter2D](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html#ga27c049795ce870216ddfb366086b5a04)(in, out, ddepth, k, anchor, delta, border);
}
};
Note how CPU (OpenCV) plugin has transformed the original kernel signature:
  * Input [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph.") has been substituted with [cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class"), holding actual input data for the underlying OpenCV function call;
  * Output [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph.") has been transformed into extra output parameter, thus `GCPUFilter2D::run()` takes one argument more than the original kernel signature.


The basic intuition for kernel developer here is _not to care_ where that [cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class") objects come from instead of the original [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph.") – and just follow the signature conventions defined by the plugin. G-API will call this method during execution and supply all the necessary information (and forward the original opaque data as-is).
#  Compound kernels
Sometimes kernel is a single thing only on API level. It is convenient for users, but on a particular implementation side it would be better to have multiple kernels (a subgraph) doing the thing instead. An example is goodFeaturesToTrack() – while in OpenCV backend it may remain a single kernel, with Fluid it becomes compound – Fluid can handle Harris response calculation but can't do sparse non-maxima suppression and point extraction to an STL vector:
