if ( right.[empty](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#abbec3525a852e77998aba034813fded4)() )
{
cout<<"Cannot read image file: "<<right_im;
return -1;
}
We start by loading the source stereopair. For this tutorial we will take a somewhat challenging example from the MPI-Sintel dataset with a lot of texture-less regions.
  2. **Prepare the views for matching**
max_disp/=2;
if(max_disp%16!=0)
max_disp += 16-(max_disp%16);
[resize](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/d54/group__imgproc__transform.html#ga47a974309e9102f5f08231edc7e7529d)(left ,left_for_matcher ,[Size](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d50/classcv_1_1Size__.html)(),0.5,0.5, INTER_LINEAR_EXACT);
[resize](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/d54/group__imgproc__transform.html#ga47a974309e9102f5f08231edc7e7529d)(right,right_for_matcher,[Size](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d50/classcv_1_1Size__.html)(),0.5,0.5, INTER_LINEAR_EXACT);
We perform downscaling of the views to speed-up the matching stage at the cost of minor quality degradation. To get the best possible quality downscaling should be avoided.
  3. **Perform matching and create the filter instance**
[Ptr<StereoBM>](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/dc/d84/group__core__basic.html#ga524e5e94ebf48db273a71ab275eaf5b5) left_matcher = StereoBM::create(max_disp,wsize);
wls_filter = [createDisparityWLSFilter](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/d17/group__ximgproc__filters.html#ga2709c56d6e1d5d9a31467cc2b85aa9cf)(left_matcher);
[Ptr<StereoMatcher>](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/dc/d84/group__core__basic.html#ga524e5e94ebf48db273a71ab275eaf5b5) right_matcher = [createRightMatcher](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/d17/group__ximgproc__filters.html#ga5c8dcf94c16eefff293311f4c5a9259d)(left_matcher);
[cvtColor](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d8/d01/group__imgproc__color__conversions.html#gaf86c09fe702ed037c03c2bc603ceab14)(left_for_matcher, left_for_matcher, COLOR_BGR2GRAY);
[cvtColor](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d8/d01/group__imgproc__color__conversions.html#gaf86c09fe702ed037c03c2bc603ceab14)(right_for_matcher, right_for_matcher, COLOR_BGR2GRAY);
matching_time = (double)[getTickCount](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/de0/group__core__utils.html#gae73f58000611a1af25dd36d496bf4487)();
left_matcher-> compute(left_for_matcher, right_for_matcher,left_disp);
right_matcher->compute(right_for_matcher,left_for_matcher, right_disp);
matching_time = ((double)[getTickCount](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/de0/group__core__utils.html#gae73f58000611a1af25dd36d496bf4487)() - matching_time)/[getTickFrequency](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/de0/group__core__utils.html#ga705441a9ef01f47acdc55d87fbe5090c)();
We are using StereoBM for faster processing. If speed is not critical, though, StereoSGBM would provide better quality. The filter instance is created by providing the StereoMatcher instance that we intend to use. Another matcher instance is returned by the createRightMatcher function. These two matcher instances are then used to compute disparity maps both for the left and right views, that are required by the filter.
  4. **Perform filtering**
wls_filter->setLambda(lambda);
wls_filter->setSigmaColor(sigma);
filtering_time = (double)[getTickCount](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/de0/group__core__utils.html#gae73f58000611a1af25dd36d496bf4487)();
wls_filter->filter(left_disp,left,filtered_disp,right_disp);
filtering_time = ((double)[getTickCount](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/de0/group__core__utils.html#gae73f58000611a1af25dd36d496bf4487)() - filtering_time)/[getTickFrequency](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/de0/group__core__utils.html#ga705441a9ef01f47acdc55d87fbe5090c)();
Disparity maps computed by the respective matcher instances, as well as the source left view are passed to the filter. Note that we are using the original non-downscaled view to guide the filtering process. The disparity map is automatically upscaled in an edge-aware fashion to match the original view resolution. The result is stored in filtered_disp.
