[cv::Mat::isContinuous](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#aa90cea495029c7d1ee0a41361ccecdf3)
bool isContinuous() const
Reports whether the matrix is continuous or not.
In case of the continuous matrix, the outer loop body is executed just once. So, the overhead is smaller, which is especially noticeable in case of small matrices.
Finally, there are STL-style iterators that are smart enough to skip gaps between successive rows: 
// compute sum of positive matrix elements, iterator-based variant
double [sum](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ga716e10a2dd9e228e4d3c95818f106722)=0;
[MatConstIterator_<double>](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/de0/classcv_1_1MatConstIterator__.html) it = M.[begin](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a5bbf4eaf8b37017dacc1e844bd643c59)<double>(), it_end = M.[end](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a51276b7653b7a0ce9aa2e69bb3601c0d)<double>();
for(; it != it_end; ++it)
[sum](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ga716e10a2dd9e228e4d3c95818f106722) += std::max(*it, 0.);
