Allocates new array data if needed. 
This is one of the key [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class") methods. Most new-style OpenCV functions and methods that produce arrays call this method for each output array. The method uses the following algorithm:
  1. If the current array shape and the type match the new ones, return immediately. Otherwise, de-reference the previous data by calling [Mat::release](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#ae48d4913285518e2c21a3457017e716e "Decrements the reference counter and deallocates the matrix if needed.").
  2. Initialize the new header.
  3. Allocate the new data of [total()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#aa4d317d43fb0cba9c2503f3c61b866c8 "Returns the total number of array elements.")*elemSize() bytes.
  4. Allocate the new, associated with the data, reference counter and set it to 1.


Such a scheme makes the memory management robust and efficient at the same time and helps avoid extra typing for you. This means that usually there is no need to explicitly allocate output arrays. That is, instead of writing: 
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) color;
