  * `d>0` is a diagonal from the upper half. For example, d=1 means the diagonal is set immediately above the main one. For example:  [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) m = ([Mat_<int>](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/dfc/classcv_1_1Mat__.html)(3,3) << 1,2,3, 4,5,6, 7,8,9); [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) d0 = m.[diag](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a024cc0510a4c61c7f266d4ab9fe13d7a)(0); [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) d1 = m.[diag](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a024cc0510a4c61c7f266d4ab9fe13d7a)(1); [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) d_1 = m.[diag](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a024cc0510a4c61c7f266d4ab9fe13d7a)(-1); Mat diag(int d=0) const Extracts a diagonal from a matrix. The resulting matrices are  d0 = [1; 5; 9] d1 = [2; 6] d_1 = [4; 8]


