##  reshape() [3/3]
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) cv::Mat::reshape  | ( | int |  _cn_ ,   
---|---|---|---  
int |  _rows_ = 0 ) const  
Changes the shape and/or the number of channels of a 2D matrix without copying the data. 
The method makes a new matrix header for *this elements. The new matrix may have a different size and/or different number of channels. Any combination is possible if:
  * No extra elements are included into the new matrix and no elements are excluded. Consequently, the product rows*cols*channels() must stay the same after the transformation.
  * No data is copied. That is, this is an O(1) operation. Consequently, if you change the number of rows, or the operation changes the indices of elements row in some other way, the matrix must be continuous. See [Mat::isContinuous](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#aa90cea495029c7d1ee0a41361ccecdf3 "Reports whether the matrix is continuous or not.") .


For example, if there is a set of 3D points stored as an STL vector, and you want to represent the points as a 3xN matrix, do the following: 
std::vector<Point3f> vec;
...
Mat pointMat = [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a2c4229732da267f1fe385458af3896d8)(vec). // convert vector to Mat, O(1) operation
[reshape](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a4eb96e3251417fa88b78e2abd6cfd7d8)(1). // make Nx3 1-channel matrix out of Nx1 3-channel.
// Also, an O(1) operation
[t](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#aaa428c60ccb6d8ea5de18f63dfac8e11)(); // finally, transpose the Nx3 matrix.
// This involves copying all the elements
[cv::Mat::reshape](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a4eb96e3251417fa88b78e2abd6cfd7d8)
Mat reshape(int cn, int rows=0) const
Changes the shape and/or the number of channels of a 2D matrix without copying the data.
MatExpr t() const
Transposes a matrix.
3-channel 2x2 matrix reshaped to 1-channel 4x3 matrix, each column has values from one of original channels: 
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) m([Size](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/dc/d84/group__core__basic.html#ga346f563897249351a34549137c8532a0)(2, 2), [CV_8UC3](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga88c4cd9de76f678f33928ef1e3f96047), [Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/dc/d84/group__core__basic.html#ga599fe92e910c027be274233eccad7beb)(1, 2, 3));
vector<int> new_shape {4, 3};
m = m.reshape(1, new_shape);
or: 
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) m([Size](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/dc/d84/group__core__basic.html#ga346f563897249351a34549137c8532a0)(2, 2), [CV_8UC3](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga88c4cd9de76f678f33928ef1e3f96047), [Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/dc/d84/group__core__basic.html#ga599fe92e910c027be274233eccad7beb)(1, 2, 3));
const int new_shape[] = {4, 3};
m = m.reshape(1, 2, new_shape); 

